#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""platform-path-lint — ratchet gates that keep producer code platform-portable.

Four rule classes share this tool because all protect the same invariant
(packages/analyzer/platform_profile.py is the ONE home for platform
divergence) and all ride the same shrink-only baseline machinery:

  paths   — hardcoded ``/Users/`` path literals (the original rule).
  blind   — platform-BLIND producers/monitors: a macOS-only binary or a
            macOS-only string baked into a user-facing Signal/Finding
            message with NO nearby platform gate. On a Linux/VPS pod these
            ``FileNotFoundError`` into false CRITICALs (the
            "macOS check fires false CRITICAL on a Linux pod" bug class).
  repo    — hardcoded ``/Users/Shared/evolve-repo`` deploy-checkout literals
            in a path-resolution context (``Path(...)`` / ``os.path.*`` /
            subprocess ``cwd=``). The deploy checkout is
            ``/var/lib/evolve/repo`` on Linux, so a macOS literal silently
            breaks path resolution on Linux pods. A narrower, HARD-BLOCK
            sibling of the paths rule: its baseline is empty (the class is
            fully platform-keyed), so ANY reintroduction is caught — even in
            a file whose paths baseline still grandfathers other ``/Users/``
            literals. Steer authors to
            ``platform_profile.get_profile().deploy_checkout_default``.
  binpath — hardcoded COMMAND-BINARY path literals that live at a
            DIFFERENT absolute path per platform: ``/usr/sbin/chown`` (Linux
            ``/usr/bin/chown``), ``/usr/sbin/lsof`` (Linux
            ``/usr/bin/lsof``), and any absolute openclaw CLI path (macOS
            Homebrew symlink vs Linux ``/usr/bin/openclaw`` vs the
            node_modules entrypoints). Scoped to the admin daemon package
            (``packages/admin/evolve_admin``). The bug is silent and severe:
            the ``evolve`` NOPASSWD sudoers grant is rendered with the
            platform-correct binary, so on a Linux pod a ``sudo
            /usr/sbin/chown …`` argv does NOT match the grant → sudo asks for
            a password → no TTY on the daemon → the op fails, often leaving a
            file root-owned and unreadable by the bot (live VPS incident:
            approving a Telegram user left the bot creds root-owned →
            gateway EACCES; a second: bot_forge's hardcoded
            ``/opt/homebrew/bin/openclaw`` failed 4/4 Linux forge installs).
            Route the binary through
            ``platform_profile.get_profile().chown`` / ``.lsof``, and
            openclaw through ``platform_profile.find_openclaw_cli()``.

``--rule {paths,blind,repo,binpath,all}`` selects; the default is ``all`` so
a bare ``--all`` (the CI invocation) runs every rule. The pre-commit hook
runs the fast, low-false-positive rules (``blind``, ``repo``, ``binpath``)
in ``--staged`` mode; the broader ``paths`` rule stays CI-only.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
paths rule (8.3 Linux port, docs/design-linux-port-2026-06-10.md §6)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Every platform-divergent path lives in ``platform_profile.py`` and every
home-directory lookup goes through the blessed helpers
(``evolve_config.bot_home`` / ``user_home`` — pwd-first resolution; never
``f"/Users/{bot_id}"`` path math, because bot_id ≠ OS account name).

This gate freezes the current per-file count of ``/Users/`` string
literals in tools/platform-path-baseline.txt, BLOCKS any change that adds
a new one, and lets the baseline ratchet DOWN as files are migrated.

Detection is AST-based: every ``str`` constant containing ``/Users/``
counts once per occurrence — f-string fragments included. Comments and
docstrings do NOT count (comments are invisible to the AST; docstrings are
prose, and prose may legitimately mention macOS paths).

To fix a paths hit, in order of preference:
  1. home lookup for a logical bot id      → evolve_config.bot_home(bot_id)
  2. home lookup for a resolved OS account → evolve_config.user_home(user)
  3. platform default (shared dir, deploy checkout, daemon dir,
     command path)                         → platform_profile.get_profile()

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
blind rule (platform-blind producer guard)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Producers/monitors under ``packages/analyzer/`` emit Signals/Findings. Some
hardcode a macOS-only binary or a macOS-only message string with no platform
branch; on a Linux pod the binary is absent, the subprocess raises, and the
monitor reports a false CRITICAL. This rule flags, when NOT inside a
function that consults a platform gate:

  - a subprocess invocation (``subprocess.run/Popen/call/check_call/
    check_output``) whose argv names a macOS-only tool — socketfilterfw,
    pfctl, dscl, fdesetup, softwareupdate, csrutil, spctl, scutil,
    system_profiler, sw_vers, diskutil, or any
    ``/usr/libexec/ApplicationFirewall/*`` path. ``launchctl``/``systemctl``
    are deliberately NOT here — those go through the scheduler seam, which
    ``scheduler-factory-lint`` owns.
  - a ``"macOS"`` / ``"darwin"`` / ``"FileVault"`` substring (case-
    insensitive) embedded in a ``message=`` / ``body=`` / ``title=`` keyword
    argument — the user-facing text of a Finding/Signal.

"Guarded" (proximity heuristic) = the offending call/literal is lexically
inside a function whose body anywhere references ``get_profile`` OR a raw
``sys.platform`` / ``platform.system()`` check. A raw platform check is
accepted here because it correctly skips the macOS path on Linux (it
prevents the false CRITICAL, which is THIS rule's concern); the paths rule
still steers new path code toward the blessed accessor.

Scope is ``packages/analyzer/`` minus the per-OS seam adapters
(``runtime/`` isolation/perms/scheduler, selected by a factory and thus
legitimately macOS-specific), the platform home itself, and in-bot
application code (``evolve_apps/``). Counts are frozen per-file in
tools/platform-blind-baseline.txt and may only ratchet DOWN.

To fix a blind hit: wrap the macOS-only path in
``if get_profile().name == "macos":`` (return a ``skipped`` Finding /
no Signal on other platforms), or add the Linux equivalent behind the
matching branch. If a macOS-only message is correct only on macOS, gate
the whole check. If a hit is genuinely unavoidable, bump
tools/platform-blind-baseline.txt in this PR so the exception is reviewable.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
repo rule (deploy-checkout-path guard)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
The deploy checkout — the read-only repo the daemons load code from — is
``/Users/Shared/evolve-repo`` on macOS but ``/var/lib/evolve/repo`` on
Linux. A hardcoded macOS literal in a path-resolution position fails on a
Linux pod: the path does not exist, so a rollback safety net no-ops, a
monitor goes blind, a runner script is never found. This rule flags a
``str`` constant containing ``/Users/Shared/evolve-repo`` when it is:

  - a positional argument to ``Path(...)`` (or ``PurePath`` / ``PosixPath`` /
    ``PurePosixPath``),
  - a positional argument to an ``os.path.*`` helper, or
  - the value of a ``cwd=`` keyword argument.

We match the FULL deploy-checkout literal, not a bare ``evolve-repo``
substring, so unrelated uses (the SSH deploy-key path
``/Users/evolve/.ssh/evolve-repo``, an ``evolve-repo-backup`` sibling) are
not false-flagged. Comments and docstrings do not count (AST-invisible /
prose). Operator-recipe strings, log lines, and help text are not path
resolution and are out of scope (the paths rule still counts them).

To fix a repo hit: resolve the deploy checkout from the platform profile —
``from platform_profile import get_profile`` then
``Path(get_profile().deploy_checkout_default)`` (see
``repo_puller.DEFAULT_REPO`` for the reference usage). Counts are frozen
per-file in tools/platform-repo-path-baseline.txt and may only ratchet
DOWN; the goal state is empty.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
binpath rule (macOS command-binary path guard)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
``chown`` and ``lsof`` exist on Linux but at a DIFFERENT absolute path than
on macOS (``/usr/sbin/chown`` → ``/usr/bin/chown``; ``/usr/sbin/lsof`` →
``/usr/bin/lsof``). The admin daemon runs these under ``sudo`` against a
NOPASSWD grant that is itself rendered from the platform profile
(``setup_wizard._render_evolve_sudoers`` uses ``get_profile().chown`` /
``.lsof``). A hardcoded macOS path in the daemon's argv therefore fails to
match the Linux grant: sudo prompts for a password, the daemon has no TTY,
and the chown/lsof silently fails (a chown often leaves a token file
root-owned and unreadable by the bot user).

The openclaw CLI is the same class: its ``evolve ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:
SETENV: <path>`` grant is baked from
``platform_profile.find_openclaw_cli()`` at setup time, and the binary
lives at a different path per platform/install (Homebrew symlink,
``/usr/bin/openclaw``, node_modules entrypoints). A hardcoded absolute
openclaw path in daemon argv can only ever match one platform's grant —
bot_forge's ``/opt/homebrew/bin/openclaw`` constant killed every forge
install on the Linux VPS ("sudo: a password is required", 2026-07-11). All
six resolver candidates are needled; the fix is
``platform_profile.find_openclaw_cli()`` (or ``deploy._openclaw_bin()``),
resolved at call time.

This rule counts every NON-docstring string constant whose value is
EXACTLY one of the needled binary paths, anywhere under
``packages/admin/evolve_admin`` — argv lists AND the common ``cmd = [...];
subprocess.run(cmd)`` form alike (an exact-match keeps embedded prose like
``f"sudo /usr/sbin/chown …"`` and comments out of scope). Two data-declaration modules that legitimately NAME
the macOS path — ``applications/env_portability_lint.py`` (its skip-list of
common system paths) and ``evo/inspector.py`` (the evo sandbox's macOS
allow-list) — are allowlisted, not baselined.

To fix a binpath hit: ``from platform_profile import get_profile`` then use
``get_profile().chown`` / ``get_profile().lsof`` in the argv, or for the
openclaw CLI ``platform_profile.find_openclaw_cli()`` (admin code typically
via the cached ``deploy._openclaw_bin()``) — in every case resolve at
call time, not module import, so a test's ``set_profile`` / filesystem
patching takes effect. A
bot account's PRIMARY group ``:staff`` stays a literal — it exists on Ubuntu
(gid 50); only the gid-0 ADMIN group (``wheel`` → ``root``) routes through
``get_profile().admin_group``. Counts are frozen per-file in
tools/platform-binpath-baseline.txt and may only ratchet DOWN; goal: empty.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Usage:
  tools/platform-path-lint <file.py> [...]            # lint specific files
  tools/platform-path-lint --all                      # lint all in scope
  tools/platform-path-lint --staged                   # lint git-staged
  tools/platform-path-lint --rule blind --staged      # one rule only
  tools/platform-path-lint --rule repo --staged        # deploy-checkout only
  tools/platform-path-lint --rule binpath --staged     # macOS command binary
  tools/platform-path-lint --all --update-baseline    # re-freeze (shrink)

Exit status: 0 if no file exceeds its baseline; 1 if a new literal/blind
producer appeared.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import ast
import os
import subprocess
import sys
from pathlib import Path

REPO_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent

# ── paths rule config ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PATHS_BASELINE_PATH = REPO_ROOT / "tools" / "platform-path-baseline.txt"

NEEDLE = "/Users/"

# Production code only — test files (and their golden fixtures) routinely
# pin macOS-shaped paths as expected values; including them would bloat
# the baseline without protecting the runtime.
_LINT_ROOTS = ("packages",)

# Files allowed to contain /Users/ literals forever (never baselined):
# the blessed profile home itself.
_ALLOWLIST = {
    "packages/analyzer/platform_profile.py",
}

# ── blind rule config ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
BLIND_BASELINE_PATH = REPO_ROOT / "tools" / "platform-blind-baseline.txt"

# Root the blind --all sweep walks: pod producers/monitors live here.
_BLIND_ROOT = "packages/analyzer"

# Subtrees/files under _BLIND_ROOT excluded from the blind rule. These are
# legitimately platform-specific (a per-OS seam adapter selected by a
# factory, with a Linux sibling) — flagging the macOS branch of code that
# already HAS a Linux branch would be noise.
_BLIND_EXCLUDE = (
    "packages/analyzer/platform_profile.py",  # the platform home itself
    "packages/analyzer/runtime/",             # isolation/perms/scheduler seam adapters
)

# evolve_apps/ used to be excluded WHOLESALE ("in-bot application code, not
# pod monitors"). That blanket carve-out let any pod-scheduled DAEMON living
# under evolve_apps/ dodge this gate — security-cve-scan/finalize.py is
# installed as the ``ai.evolve.evolve.security-cve-scan-finalize`` launchd /
# systemd unit, runs as ``evolve``, and shelled out to the macOS-only
# ``sw_vers`` with no platform branch (a textbook blind hit). It is NOT
# in-bot code. So the default is now IN SCOPE: every file under evolve_apps/
# is linted by the blind rule unless explicitly exempted below.
#
# A genuinely in-bot script — one that runs only inside a bot's openclaw
# workspace, on the same OS as the pod, and never emits a pod Signal/Finding
# (so the "false CRITICAL on a Linux pod" failure mode can't apply) — may be
# exempted here with a one-line justification. A pod-installed daemon must
# NEVER be added: the meta-test
# ``test_evolve_apps_pod_daemons_are_blind_in_scope`` derives the pod-daemon
# set from deploy.py's installers and fails if any of them lands in this list.
# Empty today — no shipped evolve_apps/ script is bot-workspace-only.
_BLIND_INBOT_EXEMPT: tuple[str, ...] = ()

# macOS-only CLI tools: a subprocess argv element whose basename is one of
# these (or any /usr/libexec/ApplicationFirewall/* path) FileNotFound-errors
# on a Linux pod. launchctl/systemctl are intentionally ABSENT — those go
# through the scheduler seam (scheduler-factory-lint owns them).
_MACOS_BINARIES = frozenset({
    "socketfilterfw", "pfctl", "dscl", "fdesetup", "softwareupdate",
    "csrutil", "spctl", "scutil", "system_profiler", "sw_vers", "diskutil",
})
_APPLICATION_FIREWALL_PREFIX = "/usr/libexec/ApplicationFirewall/"

# Substrings (case-insensitive) that are wrong on a Linux pod when baked
# into a user-facing message. Checked inside message/body/title kwargs only.
_MACOS_MESSAGE_TOKENS = ("macos", "darwin", "filevault")
_MESSAGE_KWARGS = frozenset({"message", "body", "title"})

# subprocess entrypoints whose first positional arg is the argv.
_SUBPROC_METHODS = frozenset({"run", "Popen", "call", "check_call", "check_output"})

# ── repo rule config ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
REPO_BASELINE_PATH = REPO_ROOT / "tools" / "platform-repo-path-baseline.txt"

# The macOS deploy-checkout literal. A NEW occurrence in a path-resolution
# context (Path()/os.path.*/cwd=) is a hard block: on Linux the deploy
# checkout is /var/lib/evolve/repo, so a macOS literal breaks path
# resolution there. Match the FULL literal (not a bare "evolve-repo"
# substring) so the SSH deploy-key path /Users/evolve/.ssh/evolve-repo and
# similar non-checkout uses are not false-flagged.
_REPO_NEEDLE = "/Users/Shared/evolve-repo"

# Path constructors whose positional string args are path resolution.
_PATH_CTORS = frozenset({"Path", "PurePath", "PosixPath", "PurePosixPath"})

# ── binpath rule config ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
BINPATH_BASELINE_PATH = REPO_ROOT / "tools" / "platform-binpath-baseline.txt"

# Root the binpath sweep walks: the admin daemon package. The daemon runs as
# the `evolve` user with no TTY, so a sudo argv that misses the NOPASSWD grant
# (because the binary path is the macOS one) fails silently here.
_BINPATH_ROOT = "packages/admin/evolve_admin"

# macOS command-binary absolute paths that live elsewhere on Linux. EXACT
# (whole-string) match — an embedded fragment like "sudo /usr/sbin/chown -R"
# is operator-recipe prose, not a binary token, so it is out of scope.
#
# The openclaw CLI paths are the same bug class with a twist: the binary
# lives at a DIFFERENT path per platform (macOS Homebrew symlink vs Linux
# /usr/bin symlink vs the node_modules entrypoints), and the sudoers SETENV
# grant is rendered from platform_profile.find_openclaw_cli() at setup time
# — so ANY hardcoded absolute openclaw path in daemon argv can't match the
# grant on the other platform (live incident: bot_forge's hardcoded
# /opt/homebrew/bin/openclaw killed every Linux forge dispatch with "sudo: a
# password is required"). All six resolver candidates are needled: route
# through platform_profile.find_openclaw_cli() (admin code typically via
# deploy._openclaw_bin()), resolved at call time.
_BINPATH_NEEDLES = frozenset({
    "/usr/sbin/chown",   # Linux: /usr/bin/chown
    "/usr/sbin/lsof",    # Linux: /usr/bin/lsof
    # openclaw CLI — the six platform_profile.OPENCLAW_CLI_CANDIDATES:
    "/opt/homebrew/bin/openclaw",
    "/usr/local/bin/openclaw",
    "/usr/bin/openclaw",
    "/opt/homebrew/lib/node_modules/openclaw/bin/openclaw",
    "/usr/local/lib/node_modules/openclaw/bin/openclaw",
    "/usr/lib/node_modules/openclaw/bin/openclaw",
})

# Files that legitimately NAME a macOS binary path as DATA (not as a
# subprocess invocation), so routing through platform_profile would be wrong —
# the binpath analogue of the paths rule's platform_profile.py allowlist:
#   env_portability_lint.py — a skip-list of common system paths the lint
#     must spell out to recognise them.
#   evo/inspector.py — the evo sandbox's macOS full-path allow-list (a
#     genuinely macOS-only DATA table; making it platform-aware is a separate
#     evo-on-Linux concern, deliberately out of this rule's scope).
_BINPATH_ALLOWLIST = {
    # The blessed platform home — the ONE place the macOS binary paths are
    # DEFINED (chown="/usr/sbin/chown", lsof="/usr/sbin/lsof"). Outside the
    # admin scope this rule walks, but named explicitly by the lint's own
    # self-test, so allowlist it the way the paths rule's _ALLOWLIST does.
    "packages/analyzer/platform_profile.py",
    "packages/admin/evolve_admin/applications/env_portability_lint.py",
    "packages/admin/evolve_admin/evo/inspector.py",
}


def _is_test_path(rel: str) -> bool:
    parts = rel.replace("\\", "/").split("/")
    if any(p in ("tests", "test") for p in parts):
        return True
    base = parts[-1]
    return base.startswith("test_") or base == "conftest.py"


def _docstring_nodes(tree: ast.AST) -> set[int]:
    """ids of Constant nodes that are docstrings (module/class/def lead)."""
    out: set[int] = set()
    for node in ast.walk(tree):
        if isinstance(
            node, (ast.Module, ast.ClassDef, ast.FunctionDef, ast.AsyncFunctionDef)
        ):
            body = getattr(node, "body", [])
            if (
                body
                and isinstance(body[0], ast.Expr)
                and isinstance(body[0].value, ast.Constant)
                and isinstance(body[0].value.value, str)
            ):
                out.add(id(body[0].value))
    return out


# ── paths rule ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def _literal_count(path: Path) -> int:
    """Occurrences of /Users/ across non-docstring string constants."""
    try:
        tree = ast.parse(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (SyntaxError, UnicodeDecodeError, ValueError):
        return 0
    docstrings = _docstring_nodes(tree)
    n = 0
    for node in ast.walk(tree):
        if (
            isinstance(node, ast.Constant)
            and isinstance(node.value, str)
            and id(node) not in docstrings
        ):
            n += node.value.count(NEEDLE)
    return n


def _iter_production_files() -> list[Path]:
    out: list[Path] = []
    for root in _LINT_ROOTS:
        for dirpath, _dirs, files in os.walk(REPO_ROOT / root):
            if "__pycache__" in dirpath:
                continue
            for f in files:
                if not f.endswith(".py"):
                    continue
                p = Path(dirpath) / f
                rel = str(p.relative_to(REPO_ROOT))
                if _is_test_path(rel) or rel in _ALLOWLIST:
                    continue
                out.append(p)
    return out


def _staged_py_files() -> list[Path]:
    r = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "diff", "--cached", "--name-only", "--diff-filter=ACM"],
        cwd=REPO_ROOT, capture_output=True, text=True,
    )
    out = []
    for name in r.stdout.splitlines():
        if name.endswith(".py") and not _is_test_path(name) and name not in _ALLOWLIST:
            p = REPO_ROOT / name
            if p.exists():
                out.append(p)
    return out


# ── blind rule ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def _blind_excluded(rel: str) -> bool:
    rel = rel.replace("\\", "/")
    if any(rel == e or rel.startswith(e) for e in _BLIND_EXCLUDE):
        return True
    # evolve_apps/ is in scope by default (pod daemons must be linted); only an
    # explicitly-listed genuinely-in-bot script is exempt.
    return rel in _BLIND_INBOT_EXEMPT


def _is_guard_node(node: ast.AST) -> bool:
    """True if *node* is a platform-gate reference: ``get_profile`` (the
    blessed accessor, as a bare name or attribute), ``sys.platform``, or
    ``platform.system()``/``machine()``/``uname()``."""
    if isinstance(node, ast.Name) and node.id == "get_profile":
        return True
    if isinstance(node, ast.Attribute):
        if node.attr == "get_profile":
            return True
        if (
            node.attr == "platform"
            and isinstance(node.value, ast.Name)
            and node.value.id == "sys"
        ):
            return True
        if (
            node.attr in ("system", "machine", "uname")
            and isinstance(node.value, ast.Name)
            and node.value.id == "platform"
        ):
            return True
    return False


def _scope_has_guard(fn: ast.AST) -> bool:
    return any(_is_guard_node(n) for n in ast.walk(fn))


def _is_subprocess_call(node: ast.Call) -> bool:
    fn = node.func
    if isinstance(fn, ast.Attribute) and fn.attr in _SUBPROC_METHODS:
        return isinstance(fn.value, ast.Name) and fn.value.id == "subprocess"
    return isinstance(fn, ast.Name) and fn.id in _SUBPROC_METHODS


def _argv_strings(argv: ast.AST) -> list[str]:
    """String literals in a subprocess argv (a list/tuple, or a bare str)."""
    if isinstance(argv, (ast.List, ast.Tuple)):
        elts = argv.elts
    elif isinstance(argv, ast.Constant) and isinstance(argv.value, str):
        elts = [argv]
    else:
        return []
    return [
        e.value for e in elts
        if isinstance(e, ast.Constant) and isinstance(e.value, str)
    ]


def _macos_binary(s: str) -> str | None:
    if s.startswith(_APPLICATION_FIREWALL_PREFIX):
        return s.rsplit("/", 1)[-1] or "ApplicationFirewall"
    base = s.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
    return base if base in _MACOS_BINARIES else None


def _string_fragments(value: ast.AST) -> list[str]:
    if isinstance(value, ast.Constant) and isinstance(value.value, str):
        return [value.value]
    if isinstance(value, ast.JoinedStr):
        return [
            v.value for v in value.values
            if isinstance(v, ast.Constant) and isinstance(v.value, str)
        ]
    return []


def _blind_violations(path: Path) -> list[tuple[int, str, str]]:
    """List of (lineno, kind, detail) for unguarded macOS-only producer
    code. kind ∈ {"binary", "message"}."""
    try:
        tree = ast.parse(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (SyntaxError, UnicodeDecodeError, ValueError):
        return []
    out: list[tuple[int, str, str]] = []

    def check_call(node: ast.Call) -> None:
        if _is_subprocess_call(node) and node.args:
            for s in _argv_strings(node.args[0]):
                b = _macos_binary(s)
                if b:
                    out.append((node.lineno, "binary", b))
                    break
        for kw in node.keywords:
            if kw.arg in _MESSAGE_KWARGS:
                for frag in _string_fragments(kw.value):
                    low = frag.lower()
                    if any(tok in low for tok in _MACOS_MESSAGE_TOKENS):
                        out.append((node.lineno, "message", kw.arg or "?"))
                        break

    def visit(node: ast.AST, guarded: bool) -> None:
        if isinstance(node, (ast.FunctionDef, ast.AsyncFunctionDef)):
            guarded = guarded or _scope_has_guard(node)
        if not guarded and isinstance(node, ast.Call):
            check_call(node)
        for child in ast.iter_child_nodes(node):
            visit(child, guarded)

    visit(tree, False)
    return out


def _iter_blind_files() -> list[Path]:
    out: list[Path] = []
    for dirpath, _dirs, files in os.walk(REPO_ROOT / _BLIND_ROOT):
        if "__pycache__" in dirpath:
            continue
        for f in files:
            if not f.endswith(".py"):
                continue
            p = Path(dirpath) / f
            rel = str(p.relative_to(REPO_ROOT))
            if _is_test_path(rel) or _blind_excluded(rel):
                continue
            out.append(p)
    return out


def _staged_blind_files() -> list[Path]:
    r = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "diff", "--cached", "--name-only", "--diff-filter=ACM"],
        cwd=REPO_ROOT, capture_output=True, text=True,
    )
    out = []
    for name in r.stdout.splitlines():
        norm = name.replace("\\", "/")
        if not norm.endswith(".py") or not norm.startswith(_BLIND_ROOT + "/"):
            continue
        if _is_test_path(norm) or _blind_excluded(norm):
            continue
        p = REPO_ROOT / name
        if p.exists():
            out.append(p)
    return out


# ── repo rule ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def _is_path_resolution_context(parent: ast.AST | None, node: ast.Constant) -> str | None:
    """Return a short context label if *node* (a str Constant) sits in a
    path-resolution position within its AST *parent*, else None.

    Path-resolution positions (the deploy-checkout-path contract):
      • a positional arg to Path()/PurePath()/PosixPath()/PurePosixPath()
      • a positional arg to an os.path.* helper (join/exists/isdir/...)
      • the value of a cwd= keyword (subprocess.run/Popen/..., or any call)
    """
    if isinstance(parent, ast.keyword) and parent.arg == "cwd" and parent.value is node:
        return "cwd="
    if isinstance(parent, ast.Call) and node in parent.args:
        fn = parent.func
        if isinstance(fn, ast.Name) and fn.id in _PATH_CTORS:
            return f"{fn.id}()"
        if isinstance(fn, ast.Attribute) and fn.attr in _PATH_CTORS:
            return f"{fn.attr}()"  # e.g. pathlib.Path("...")
        if (
            isinstance(fn, ast.Attribute)
            and isinstance(fn.value, ast.Attribute)
            and fn.value.attr == "path"
            and isinstance(fn.value.value, ast.Name)
            and fn.value.value.id == "os"
        ):
            return f"os.path.{fn.attr}()"
    return None


def _repo_violations(path: Path) -> list[tuple[int, str]]:
    """List of (lineno, context) for /Users/Shared/evolve-repo literals used
    in a path-resolution context (Path()/os.path.*/cwd=). Docstrings and
    comments are excluded (prose / AST-invisible)."""
    try:
        tree = ast.parse(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (SyntaxError, UnicodeDecodeError, ValueError):
        return []
    docstrings = _docstring_nodes(tree)
    parents: dict[int, ast.AST] = {}
    for n in ast.walk(tree):
        for c in ast.iter_child_nodes(n):
            parents[id(c)] = n
    out: list[tuple[int, str]] = []
    for node in ast.walk(tree):
        if (
            isinstance(node, ast.Constant)
            and isinstance(node.value, str)
            and _REPO_NEEDLE in node.value
            and id(node) not in docstrings
        ):
            ctx = _is_path_resolution_context(parents.get(id(node)), node)
            if ctx:
                out.append((node.lineno, ctx))
    return out


# ── binpath rule ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def _binpath_excluded(rel: str) -> bool:
    rel = rel.replace("\\", "/")
    return rel in _BINPATH_ALLOWLIST


def _binpath_violations(path: Path) -> list[tuple[int, str]]:
    """List of (lineno, binary) for non-docstring string constants whose value
    is EXACTLY a macOS command-binary path that differs on Linux. Docstrings
    (prose) and comments (AST-invisible) do not count; an embedded fragment
    such as ``f"sudo /usr/sbin/chown -R …"`` does not count either (the
    constant must equal the binary path)."""
    try:
        tree = ast.parse(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (SyntaxError, UnicodeDecodeError, ValueError):
        return []
    docstrings = _docstring_nodes(tree)
    out: list[tuple[int, str]] = []
    for node in ast.walk(tree):
        if (
            isinstance(node, ast.Constant)
            and isinstance(node.value, str)
            and node.value in _BINPATH_NEEDLES
            and id(node) not in docstrings
        ):
            out.append((node.lineno, node.value))
    return out


def _iter_binpath_files() -> list[Path]:
    out: list[Path] = []
    for dirpath, _dirs, files in os.walk(REPO_ROOT / _BINPATH_ROOT):
        if "__pycache__" in dirpath:
            continue
        for f in files:
            if not f.endswith(".py"):
                continue
            p = Path(dirpath) / f
            rel = str(p.relative_to(REPO_ROOT))
            if _is_test_path(rel) or _binpath_excluded(rel):
                continue
            out.append(p)
    return out


def _staged_binpath_files() -> list[Path]:
    r = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "diff", "--cached", "--name-only", "--diff-filter=ACM"],
        cwd=REPO_ROOT, capture_output=True, text=True,
    )
    out = []
    for name in r.stdout.splitlines():
        norm = name.replace("\\", "/")
        if not norm.endswith(".py") or not norm.startswith(_BINPATH_ROOT + "/"):
            continue
        if _is_test_path(norm) or _binpath_excluded(norm):
            continue
        p = REPO_ROOT / name
        if p.exists():
            out.append(p)
    return out


# ── baseline I/O (shared) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def _load_baseline(path: Path) -> dict[str, int]:
    if not path.exists():
        return {}
    baseline: dict[str, int] = {}
    for line in path.read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines():
        line = line.strip()
        if not line or line.startswith("#"):
            continue
        parts = line.split("\t", 1)  # "<count>\t<path>"
        if len(parts) != 2:
            continue
        try:
            baseline[parts[1]] = int(parts[0])
        except ValueError:
            continue
    return baseline


